  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As a weak upper level trough exits the eastern seaboard \ntoday, it will be replaced by an upper level ridge with high \npressure and southerly flow at the surface. Center of the SFC\nslides off the coast near the VA/NC border this afternoon. This\nwill keep conditions dry but we will see a return of warmer \ntemperatures and higher humidities each day, culminating with \nsome very warm temperatures by the end of the week.\n\nFor reference, normal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, \nand forecast highs are in the mid to upper 80s for much of ENC, with \nsome 90s possible inland THU, likely low 90s MaxTs FRI. Next\niteration of AFD will include CLIMATE section for FRI's record \nhighs. Persistent southerly flow and increasing low- level \nmoisture should help to keep overnight lows higher as well, and \nwell above the normal lows of around 60.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return for the weekend as\nGulf moisture opens up and a cold front drapes across the \nsouthern Appalachians and pushes southeastward slowly on\nSaturday. This will squeeze moisture into eastern NC elevating \nrain chances for the weekend. While thunderstorms will be \npossible, the threat for severe weather is low at this time.\n\nThe front loses steam as it approaches the coast, potentially\nbriefly stalling over Coastal Carolinas, ahead of a second \nreinforcing front spurred from the Great Lakes by a stronger\ntrough aloft.\n\nFor now, ensemble guidance is showing about a 30-50% chance of \nexceeding 1\" of rain with this system. Any rain will be helpful, \nespecially for the coastal plain of ENC that is currently in \nmoderate to extreme drought.",
